---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import InlineCommand from "../../components/InlineCommand";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import TerminalBlock from "../../components/TerminalBlock";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Code Review - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="practical-exercise" subChapterId="code-review">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">

      <p>
        AI shifts your time from <em>writing</em> code to <em>reviewing</em> code. The code ships under your name regardless of who generated it, so the diff is where your judgment matters most. This exercise gives you practice catching problems that AI introduces quietly.
      </p>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">Exercise 1: The Insecure Prompt</h2>
        <p>
          In this exercise, you'll give the AI a prompt that will produce code with a security issue. Your job is to find it in the diff before you commit.
        </p>

        <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
          <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">1</div>
          <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Give the AI a Form Task</h3>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
            Open a <strong>fresh chat session</strong> and ask the AI to build a feedback form for the dashboard. Keep the prompt intentionally light on security requirements.
          </p>
          <TerminalBlock
            title="Your Prompt"
            lines={[
              { type: "command", command: "Add a feedback form to the dashboard. It should have a text input for the user's name, a textarea for their message, and a submit button. When submitted, display the feedback on the page in a list below the form so users can see previous submissions." },
            ]}
            client:visible
          />
          <Callout variant="info" title="Why This Prompt?">
            <p>
              This prompt asks for user input to be displayed on the page without mentioning sanitization, escaping, or any security considerations. AI tends to take the shortest path to a working feature, and unless told otherwise, it may render user input directly into the DOM.
            </p>
          </Callout>
        </div>

        <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
          <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">2</div>
          <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Review the Diff</h3>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
            Before committing, review exactly what the AI generated.
          </p>
          <TerminalBlock
            title="Review Changes"
            lines={[
              { type: "command", command: "git diff" },
            ]}
            client:visible
          />
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mt-4">
            Read through the diff line by line. Look for:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-stone-600">
            <li>How is user input being stored?</li>
            <li>How is it being rendered? Is it using <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code>, <code>innerHTML</code>, or an equivalent?</li>
            <li>Is there any input sanitization or length limiting?</li>
            <li>Could a user submit <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags or HTML that would execute when displayed?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
          <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">3</div>
          <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Identify the Issue</h3>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
            Depending on the AI's output, you may find one or more of the following:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-stone-600">
            <li><strong>XSS vulnerability:</strong> User-supplied text rendered without escaping, allowing script injection.</li>
            <li><strong>No input length limits:</strong> A user could submit megabytes of text.</li>
            <li><strong>No empty submission handling:</strong> The form may accept blank entries.</li>
          </ul>

          <Callout variant="warning" title="React's Built-in Protection">
            <p>
              React escapes content rendered via JSX by default, so if the AI used React properly you may not find an XSS issue. That's fine. The point is that you <em>checked</em>. In frameworks without automatic escaping, or if the AI used <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code>, the vulnerability would be real. The habit of reviewing for these issues matters regardless of the framework.
            </p>
          </Callout>
        </div>

        <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
          <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">4</div>
          <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Fix and Clean Up</h3>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
            If you found issues, ask the AI to fix them in the same session. Be specific about what you found.
          </p>
          <TerminalBlock
            title="Example Fix Prompt"
            lines={[
              { type: "command", command: "The feedback form doesn't limit input length and has no empty submission handling. Add a max length of 500 characters to the message field and prevent submission if either field is empty." },
            ]}
            client:visible
          />
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mt-4">
            Once you're satisfied, revert the changes. This was a review exercise, not a feature we're keeping.
          </p>
          <TerminalBlock
            title="Revert"
            lines={[
              { type: "command", command: "git checkout ." },
            ]}
            client:visible
          />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">Exercise 2: Reading the Diff as Your Primary Job</h2>
        <p>
          For this exercise, go back to your feature work. Pick the next task from one of your task breakdowns and implement it normally with the AI. But before committing, treat the diff as the deliverable, not the running app.
        </p>

        <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
          <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">1</div>
          <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Implement a Task</h3>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
            Use your normal workflow: reference the task breakdown, let the AI implement, verify the feature works in the browser.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
          <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">2</div>
          <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Review Every Line of the Diff</h3>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
            Before committing, read the full diff. You can use the terminal or your IDE's diff view.
          </p>
          <TerminalBlock
            title="Review"
            lines={[
              { type: "command", command: "git diff" },
            ]}
            client:visible
          />
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mt-4">
            As you read, ask yourself:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-stone-600">
            <li>Do I understand what every changed line does?</li>
            <li>Are there edge cases the AI didn't handle?</li>
            <li>Did the AI add anything I didn't ask for (extra files, unused imports, unnecessary abstractions)?</li>
            <li>Does the code match the project's conventions?</li>
            <li>Would I approve this if it came in as a pull request from a colleague?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
          <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">3</div>
          <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Catch Something</h3>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
            It's rare for AI-generated code to be perfect on the first pass. Look for subtle issues:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-stone-600">
            <li>A missing edge case from the requirements (check back against your task breakdown)</li>
            <li>An unnecessary dependency or import</li>
            <li>A hardcoded value that should be configurable</li>
            <li>A state update that could cause unnecessary re-renders</li>
            <li>Error handling that silently swallows failures</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mt-4">
            If everything genuinely looks good, that's fine too. The goal is building the habit of reading the diff critically before committing.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">The Shift</h2>
        <p>
          Working with AI changes where you spend your time. Writing code gets faster; reviewing code becomes the bottleneck. This isn't a downside. Review is where you catch security issues, enforce consistency, ensure requirements are met, and maintain the quality bar. The faster the AI generates code, the more important it is that you read it carefully.
        </p>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 mt-8 bg-stone-50">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-2 uppercase tracking-wider">Review Checklist</div>
          <ul class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed space-y-2 list-none pl-0 m-0">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-stone-400 mt-0.5">&#9744;</span> I understand every line of the diff</li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-stone-400 mt-0.5">&#9744;</span> No security issues (input handling, data exposure, auth)</li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-stone-400 mt-0.5">&#9744;</span> Edge cases from the requirements are covered</li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-stone-400 mt-0.5">&#9744;</span> No unnecessary files, imports, or abstractions added</li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-stone-400 mt-0.5">&#9744;</span> Code matches project conventions</li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-stone-400 mt-0.5">&#9744;</span> I would approve this as a pull request</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="border-t border-stone-200 pt-8 mt-8">
        <p class="text-stone-800 font-medium">
          Commit your work from Exercise 2 if it passes your review, then continue with the remaining features. From this point on, treat the diff review as a required step before every commit.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
